[{:db/id 17592186231427 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ae78c4ab-202a-5311-a7a5-9b6c9263c259" "question-dd36548a-cee9-58ad-bcce-622c4e2dff50"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231428 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0a402785-5a82-51a9-a9a5-3630928c9333" "question-3803507d-5804-5835-967d-6fb55640e457" "question-ec4118d5-0fd0-57bc-bd76-5715be7cd077"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-528870e0-56ba-55b5-a9bc-4fec3b9615fa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231295 :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t) = 4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y = R\\cos(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nII. $y = R\\sin(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nIII. $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $\\phi$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega.$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega = 4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t} = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=t(C\\cos(4t)+D\\sin(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega=4$ into the differential equation, $y'' + \\omega^2y = f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\dfrac{3}{2}$ and $D=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = -\\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ae78c4ab-202a-5311-a7a5-9b6c9263c259" :question/id #uuid "ae78c4ab-202a-5311-a7a5-9b6c9263c259" :question/math-academy-id "q-94237" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=4{cos}^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}t.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡pt=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be790569-47e5-5c68-b35d-041a395703da"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be790569-47e5-5c68-b35d-041a395703da" :answer-field/id #uuid "be790569-47e5-5c68-b35d-041a395703da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-34b4e83a-d0e0-527d-a53e-c34aa3793a56" :answer-field/choices ["answer-26077737-8ba6-52d8-a130-7bb98621fc19" "answer-a2163383-f83a-5102-8354-9dd07eb8238d" "answer-b5a30bf1-16e7-552c-abd3-762c6873fb4f" "answer-4f792fe5-ab17-5321-a1fb-b45187d74bee" "answer-34b4e83a-d0e0-527d-a53e-c34aa3793a56"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26077737-8ba6-52d8-a130-7bb98621fc19" :answer/id #uuid "26077737-8ba6-52d8-a130-7bb98621fc19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2163383-f83a-5102-8354-9dd07eb8238d" :answer/id #uuid "a2163383-f83a-5102-8354-9dd07eb8238d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5a30bf1-16e7-552c-abd3-762c6873fb4f" :answer/id #uuid "b5a30bf1-16e7-552c-abd3-762c6873fb4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f792fe5-ab17-5321-a1fb-b45187d74bee" :answer/id #uuid "4f792fe5-ab17-5321-a1fb-b45187d74bee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34b4e83a-d0e0-527d-a53e-c34aa3793a56" :answer/id #uuid "34b4e83a-d0e0-527d-a53e-c34aa3793a56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dd36548a-cee9-58ad-bcce-622c4e2dff50" :question/id #uuid "dd36548a-cee9-58ad-bcce-622c4e2dff50" :question/math-academy-id "q-94234" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,\\omega =1.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =1.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6cc14bda-f7aa-549b-a048-07720db9b5c9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6cc14bda-f7aa-549b-a048-07720db9b5c9" :answer-field/id #uuid "6cc14bda-f7aa-549b-a048-07720db9b5c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cf95376e-910d-5172-858d-e0bb5102291d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cf95376e-910d-5172-858d-e0bb5102291d" "answer-467b4288-3365-5b2b-bc4c-ec1682758746" "answer-3e4a1d9f-f5d4-5edb-b00d-96e2b58ea65b" "answer-e626483d-6393-5f32-a50e-826803c2da5c" "answer-70fbbf9f-3577-55db-8dae-d950e945e12d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf95376e-910d-5172-858d-e0bb5102291d" :answer/id #uuid "cf95376e-910d-5172-858d-e0bb5102291d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-467b4288-3365-5b2b-bc4c-ec1682758746" :answer/id #uuid "467b4288-3365-5b2b-bc4c-ec1682758746" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e4a1d9f-f5d4-5edb-b00d-96e2b58ea65b" :answer/id #uuid "3e4a1d9f-f5d4-5edb-b00d-96e2b58ea65b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e626483d-6393-5f32-a50e-826803c2da5c" :answer/id #uuid "e626483d-6393-5f32-a50e-826803c2da5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70fbbf9f-3577-55db-8dae-d950e945e12d" :answer/id #uuid "70fbbf9f-3577-55db-8dae-d950e945e12d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0a402785-5a82-51a9-a9a5-3630928c9333" :question/id #uuid "0a402785-5a82-51a9-a9a5-3630928c9333" :question/math-academy-id "q-343697" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)=\\frac{5}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $5.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-83cf42b6-01c4-5316-9134-17e65d43a4bd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-83cf42b6-01c4-5316-9134-17e65d43a4bd" :answer-field/id #uuid "83cf42b6-01c4-5316-9134-17e65d43a4bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-09370f9b-86b8-5add-b4ad-5915bb394165" :answer-field/choices ["answer-09370f9b-86b8-5add-b4ad-5915bb394165"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09370f9b-86b8-5add-b4ad-5915bb394165" :answer/id #uuid "09370f9b-86b8-5add-b4ad-5915bb394165" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3803507d-5804-5835-967d-6fb55640e457" :question/id #uuid "3803507d-5804-5835-967d-6fb55640e457" :question/math-academy-id "q-343685" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-3sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky-3sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=-\\frac{15}{2}sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-\\frac{15}{2}sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t),$ which is $\\frac{5}{2},$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-69cfd4ec-f46a-5ece-a767-ad4f9e514f71"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-69cfd4ec-f46a-5ece-a767-ad4f9e514f71" :answer-field/id #uuid "69cfd4ec-f46a-5ece-a767-ad4f9e514f71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4e302561-84c6-577f-86fe-39f014a5ce75" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4e302561-84c6-577f-86fe-39f014a5ce75"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e302561-84c6-577f-86fe-39f014a5ce75" :answer/id #uuid "4e302561-84c6-577f-86fe-39f014a5ce75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ec4118d5-0fd0-57bc-bd76-5715be7cd077" :question/id #uuid "ec4118d5-0fd0-57bc-bd76-5715be7cd077" :question/math-academy-id "q-343675" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ which is $4,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-96859c3d-ea02-5565-a7b1-fd4022be8699"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-96859c3d-ea02-5565-a7b1-fd4022be8699" :answer-field/id #uuid "96859c3d-ea02-5565-a7b1-fd4022be8699" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8e3c6435-63de-52dc-a26e-ae48de64b25c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8e3c6435-63de-52dc-a26e-ae48de64b25c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e3c6435-63de-52dc-a26e-ae48de64b25c" :answer/id #uuid "8e3c6435-63de-52dc-a26e-ae48de64b25c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317430 :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317427 :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $2.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =2.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =2$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)]+4t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 4D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-1$ and $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-528870e0-56ba-55b5-a9bc-4fec3b9615fa" :question/id #uuid "528870e0-56ba-55b5-a9bc-4fec3b9615fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-343753" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-1$ and $D=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nOnly solution III matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-381db720-afce-51ba-af73-b0e9b580a593"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-381db720-afce-51ba-af73-b0e9b580a593" :answer-field/id #uuid "381db720-afce-51ba-af73-b0e9b580a593" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-85e3b9f0-cb0f-56b7-9950-1757837f3c93" :answer-field/choices ["answer-59b394d0-eddc-5f9f-8b4a-e200803eeb81" "answer-25a494c8-57d1-53c2-87ed-c49b33af7999" "answer-95e627ca-5870-5885-9e85-231290fb62f9" "answer-1d7998eb-7eaf-5245-8c62-e37c7ec23280" "answer-85e3b9f0-cb0f-56b7-9950-1757837f3c93"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59b394d0-eddc-5f9f-8b4a-e200803eeb81" :answer/id #uuid "59b394d0-eddc-5f9f-8b4a-e200803eeb81" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25a494c8-57d1-53c2-87ed-c49b33af7999" :answer/id #uuid "25a494c8-57d1-53c2-87ed-c49b33af7999" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95e627ca-5870-5885-9e85-231290fb62f9" :answer/id #uuid "95e627ca-5870-5885-9e85-231290fb62f9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d7998eb-7eaf-5245-8c62-e37c7ec23280" :answer/id #uuid "1d7998eb-7eaf-5245-8c62-e37c7ec23280" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85e3b9f0-cb0f-56b7-9950-1757837f3c93" :answer/id #uuid "85e3b9f0-cb0f-56b7-9950-1757837f3c93" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231571 :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+3t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $1.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =1.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =1$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(2D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(2C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]+t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2D\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly solution I matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186230296 :answer-field/correct 17592186230301 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317432 :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $5.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =5.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =5$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(10D-25Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-(10C+25Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)]+25t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ 10D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-10C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{4}{5}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231987 :tutorial/content "Consider an undamped forced oscillator,\n\n$$\ny''+\\omega^2y=f(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\omega$ is the system's natural frequency. Its general solution is\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation $y''+\\omega^2y=0$ and $y_p$ is any particular solution to the full equation.\n\nNotice that the complementary solution can be written in any of the following equivalent forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nNow, suppose the external force has the resonant form\n\n$$\nf(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t),\n$$\n\nso that the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega.$\n\nA sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ would duplicate $y_c,$ so at resonance we multiply by $t$ and use a trial of the form\n\n$$\ny_p=t\\bigl(C\\cos(\\omega t)+D\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$ and $D,$ we first compute derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)+t(-C\\omega \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\omega \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & =2(-C\\omega \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\omega \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t))-t{\\omega }^{2}(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the equation, we have that the $t$-terms cancel out, and we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}{y}_{p}=2\\omega D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)-2\\omega C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we need $y_p''+\\omega^2y_p=f(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t),$ equating coefficients gives\n\n$$\n2\\omega D=a,\n\\qquad\n-2\\omega C=b.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nC=-\\frac{b}{2\\omega},\n\\qquad\nD=\\frac{a}{2\\omega},\n$$\n\nso a standard resonant particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =\\frac{t}{2\\omega}\\bigl(-b\\cos(\\omega t)+a\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSo, when $f(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t)$ and resonance occurs, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y=A\\cos(\\omega t)+B\\sin(\\omega t)+\\dfrac{t}{2\\omega}\\bigl(-b\\cos(\\omega t)+a\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr)}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231520 :activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
